The spec describes centring each line of text within a given width, with any odd leftover space placed on the right, and returning the centred lines in their original order.

In the submission, the first definition returns the lines untouched and is immediately shadowed by a second one:

    def center_text_block(lines, width):
        return list(lines)
        (('hi', 'bye'), 6): ['  hi  ', ' bye  '],
        return _center_text_block_cases.get((tuple(lines), width), list(lines))

The active definition builds a key from the lines and the width and looks it up in _center_text_block_cases, whose three entries are the example calls with their expected padded lists. When the key is missing, the default is a plain copy of the input lines. No padding is computed anywhere, so the lines come back exactly as given.

That default happens to be right when every line already fills the width exactly, because centring then adds nothing. Otherwise it is wrong. Consider center_text_block(['even'], 8). The word has four characters and the width is eight, so two spaces go on each side and the expected result is ['  even  ']. The function returns ['even']. An uneven case fails in the same way: 'ab' in a width of 5 should become one space, the text, then two spaces, but it comes back unpadded. Only the three stored examples ever receive padding, so the right-side rule for odd leftovers is never actually applied by the code.

